1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,933
This is ECE 358, video 2 a. The topic is the network core, part 1.

2
00:00:07,383 --> 00:00:10,903
After this video, you can explain forwarding and routing.

3
00:00:11,353 --> 00:00:16,196
You can draw store and forward timing, with L over R for each hop.

4
00:00:16,646 --> 00:00:18,630
You can explain queueing and loss.

5
00:00:19,080 --> 00:00:23,389
And you can explain circuit switching, F D M and T D M.

6
00:00:23,839 --> 00:00:35,445
At the end, we solve question bank questions 16 to 21, and 25. Video 2 b continues with the number of users and the structure of the Internet.

7
00:00:35,895 --> 00:00:39,820
The network core is a mesh of interconnected routers.

8
00:00:40,270 --> 00:00:48,675
A mesh has many links. So more than one path can connect two hosts. Here, path 1 is blue, and path 2 is orange.

9
00:00:49,125 --> 00:00:55,120
The core uses packet switching. The source host breaks the application message into packets.

10
00:00:55,570 --> 00:01:04,679
The network forwards each packet from one router to the next, one link at a time. Here, packet 1 goes from R1 to R2.

11
00:01:05,129 --> 00:01:14,729
Each packet goes along the path until it arrives at the destination. The destination then has all 3 packets, so it has the full message.

12
00:01:15,179 --> 00:01:24,993
This is packet switching. Hosts break messages into packets. The network forwards each packet from router to router, across the links of the path.

13
00:01:25,443 --> 00:01:29,368
A router does two key functions. The first is forwarding.

14
00:01:29,818 --> 00:01:36,389
An IP packet has two parts: a header and data. The header holds the destination address.

15
00:01:36,839 --> 00:01:42,620
Each router has a forwarding table. This is the very simple table of slide 20.

16
00:01:43,070 --> 00:01:48,702
A packet arrives on input link 4. Its header value is 0 1 1 1.

17
00:01:49,152 --> 00:01:54,400
The router finds the row with the header value 0 1 1 1 in its table.

18
00:01:54,850 --> 00:01:56,877
That row gives output link 2.

19
00:01:57,327 --> 00:02:03,193
The router moves the packet from input link 4 to output link 2. This is forwarding.

20
00:02:03,643 --> 00:02:10,854
Forwarding is a local action: one router uses its own table. Forwarding is also called switching.

21
00:02:11,304 --> 00:02:16,061
A second packet arrives. Its header value is 1 0 0 1.

22
00:02:16,511 --> 00:02:24,447
The row 1 0 0 1 of the table gives output link 1. So the router sends this packet on link 1.

23
00:02:24,897 --> 00:02:28,759
The router reads only the header of a packet, not the data.

24
00:02:29,209 --> 00:02:37,699
The second function is routing. Routing is a global action. It determines the source to destination path that the packets take.

25
00:02:38,149 --> 00:02:41,648
A routing algorithm, R A, runs for the routers.

26
00:02:42,098 --> 00:02:54,130
The routing algorithm creates the forwarding tables. The slides also call them routing tables, or R Ts. So routing makes the tables, and forwarding uses them.

27
00:02:54,580 --> 00:02:59,892
Remember two facts. First, a router continually updates its routing table.

28
00:03:00,342 --> 00:03:04,993
Second, when a packet arrives, the router forwards it with the current table.

29
00:03:05,443 --> 00:03:15,704
Slides 21 and 22 show the difference on a road map. Routing chooses the whole route across the country, from San Jose to Northampton.

30
00:03:16,154 --> 00:03:23,407
Forwarding is the choice of one exit road at one junction. The driver makes that choice at every junction.

31
00:03:23,857 --> 00:03:28,209
A source sends packet 1 to a router. The packet has L bits.

32
00:03:28,659 --> 00:03:31,689
Each link has a rate of R bits per second.

33
00:03:32,139 --> 00:03:37,429
The bits go onto the link one by one. Part of packet 1 is now in the router.

34
00:03:37,879 --> 00:03:40,141
The router cannot send packet 1 yet.

35
00:03:40,591 --> 00:03:45,455
When all L bits are in the router, the router sends the packet on the next link.

36
00:03:45,905 --> 00:03:51,473
This is store and forward. The router must have the entire packet before it transmits it.

37
00:03:51,923 --> 00:04:01,395
To push L bits into a link at R bits per second takes L over R seconds. This is the packet transmission delay, d trans.

38
00:04:01,845 --> 00:04:10,783
Slide 23 uses L equals 10 kilobits. That is 10 times 10 to the power 3, or 10 to the power 4 bits.

39
00:04:11,233 --> 00:04:20,599
R equals 100 Mbps. That is 100 times 10 to the power 6, or 10 to the power 8 bits per second.

40
00:04:21,049 --> 00:04:29,561
L over R is 10 to the power 4, divided by 10 to the power 8. That is 10 to the power minus 4 seconds.

41
00:04:30,011 --> 00:04:42,448
10 to the power minus 4 seconds is 0.1 times 10 to the power minus 3 seconds, which is 0.1 ms. That is the transmission delay for one hop.

42
00:04:42,898 --> 00:04:50,834
Now follow the three packets of slide 23 over two links: from the source, to the router, to the destination.

43
00:04:51,284 --> 00:05:02,143
Time goes down. Each transmission takes L over R, which is 0.1 ms. So each row of the diagram is 0.1 ms.

44
00:05:02,593 --> 00:05:08,523
From 0 to 0.1 ms, the source transmits packet 1 on link 1.

45
00:05:08,973 --> 00:05:16,141
At 0.1 ms, the router has all of packet 1. It sends packet 1 on link 2.

46
00:05:16,591 --> 00:05:20,282
At the same time, the source sends packet 2 on link 1.

47
00:05:20,732 --> 00:05:31,420
From 0.2 to 0.3 ms, packet 2 crosses link 2 and packet 3 crosses link 1. Both links work at the same time.

48
00:05:31,870 --> 00:05:38,697
From 0.3 to 0.4 ms, the router sends packet 3 on link 2.

49
00:05:39,147 --> 00:05:43,605
The destination has all 3 packets at 0.4 ms.

50
00:05:44,055 --> 00:05:52,162
Count the total. The first link sends 3 packets: 3 times 0.1, which is 0.3 ms.

51
00:05:52,612 --> 00:06:02,809
Then the last packet needs 0.1 ms on the second link. 0.3 plus 0.1 is 0.4 ms.

52
00:06:03,259 --> 00:06:14,929
Now the source sends all 30 kilobits as one packet. One transmission takes 30 kilobits over 100 Mbps, which is 0.3 ms.

53
00:06:15,379 --> 00:06:26,835
The router must store the whole packet first. Then it needs 0.3 ms more on link 2. 2 times 0.3 is 0.6 ms.

54
00:06:27,285 --> 00:06:38,954
So the three small packets arrive at 0.4 ms, and the one big packet at 0.6 ms. Small packets let the links work in parallel.

55
00:06:39,404 --> 00:06:45,591
Hosts A and B send to the first router on links of 100 Mbps each.

56
00:06:46,041 --> 00:06:50,691
The output link of the router has only 1.5 Mbps.

57
00:06:51,141 --> 00:07:02,128
Packets from A and B arrive faster than the output link can send them. So they wait in a queue in the router. Queueing occurs when work arrives faster than it can be serviced.

58
00:07:02,578 --> 00:07:08,189
While the arrival rate stays above 1.5 Mbps, the queue grows.

59
00:07:08,639 --> 00:07:14,740
The router memory, the buffer, has a fixed size. Here it has 8 places, and all 8 are full.

60
00:07:15,190 --> 00:07:20,097
When the buffer is full, the router drops the next packet. That packet is lost.

61
00:07:20,547 --> 00:07:29,357
This is the rule of slide 25. If the arrival rate exceeds the transmission rate for some period of time, packets queue.

62
00:07:29,807 --> 00:07:32,581
If the buffer fills up, packets are dropped.

63
00:07:33,031 --> 00:07:40,967
Here is an example with numbers. They are not on the slides. For 2 seconds, A and B each send 1 megabit per second.

64
00:07:41,417 --> 00:07:45,065
The output link sends 1.5 megabits per second.

65
00:07:45,515 --> 00:07:48,139
The buffer holds 0.6 megabits.

66
00:07:48,589 --> 00:07:55,010
The arrival rate is 1 plus 1, which is 2 megabits per second. That is more than 1.5.

67
00:07:55,460 --> 00:08:01,689
So the queue grows at 2 minus 1.5, which is 0.5 megabits per second.

68
00:08:02,139 --> 00:08:09,179
The buffer is full after 0.6 divided by 0.5, which is 1.2 seconds.

69
00:08:09,629 --> 00:08:16,029
From 1.2 seconds to 2 seconds, the extra bits have no place. The router drops them.

70
00:08:16,479 --> 00:08:27,658
The loss lasts 2 minus 1.2, which is 0.8 seconds. The loss is 0.5 times 0.8, which is 0.4 megabits.

71
00:08:28,108 --> 00:08:42,252
At 2 seconds, the arrivals stop. The link empties the 0.6 megabits at 1.5 megabits per second. 0.6 divided by 1.5 is 0.4 seconds.

72
00:08:42,702 --> 00:08:49,614
Now check the numbers. 2 megabits per second arrived for 2 seconds. So 4 megabits arrived.

73
00:08:50,064 --> 00:08:57,275
The link sent 1.5 megabits per second for 2.4 seconds. That is 3.6 megabits.

74
00:08:57,725 --> 00:09:06,109
3.6 megabits sent plus 0.4 megabits lost is 4 megabits. So every arrived bit is counted.

75
00:09:06,559 --> 00:09:16,159
Circuit switching is the alternative to packet switching. In the slide diagram, each link has four circuits. A circuit is a separate channel.

76
00:09:16,609 --> 00:09:25,526
Before the call sends data, it reserves one circuit on each link of its path. In the top link, the call gets circuit 2 of 4.

77
00:09:25,976 --> 00:09:29,048
In the right link, the call gets circuit 1 of 4.

78
00:09:29,498 --> 00:09:38,458
End to end resources are allocated to and reserved for the call. Examples are buffer, link bandwidth, and processing power.

79
00:09:38,908 --> 00:09:45,905
The resources are dedicated: there is no sharing. So the call gets circuit-like, guaranteed performance.

80
00:09:46,355 --> 00:09:52,009
But when the call does not send, its circuits stay idle. No other call can use them.

81
00:09:52,459 --> 00:09:56,149
Traditional telephone networks commonly use circuit switching.

82
00:09:56,599 --> 00:10:08,077
A link can carry several circuits in two ways. The first is frequency division multiplexing, F D M. The frequencies are divided into narrow bands.

83
00:10:08,527 --> 00:10:14,372
Each call gets its own band, for all of the time. Here, call 1 has the top band.

84
00:10:14,822 --> 00:10:21,393
The second way is time division multiplexing, T D M. Time is divided into slots.

85
00:10:21,843 --> 00:10:30,675
Each call gets periodic slots: call 1 gets every fourth slot. During its slot, the call uses the whole, wider band.

86
00:10:31,125 --> 00:10:38,122
So in F D M, each call transmits all the time, at the rate of its narrow band: R over N.

87
00:10:38,572 --> 00:10:44,865
In T D M, each call transmits at the full rate R, but only in 1 slot of N.

88
00:10:45,315 --> 00:10:51,779
For example, a link of R equals 4 Mbps carries N equals 4 calls.

89
00:10:52,229 --> 00:11:00,421
With F D M, each band gets R over N, which is 4 over 4, or 1 Mbps, all the time.

90
00:11:00,871 --> 00:11:12,455
With T D M, a call sends at 4 Mbps, but only in 1 slot of 4. On average, 4 times one quarter is 1 Mbps.

91
00:11:12,905 --> 00:11:22,057
Both give 1 Mbps on average. G S M mobile networks use a combination of F D M and T D M.

92
00:11:22,507 --> 00:11:31,062
Now the questions. Question bank questions 16, 17 and 18 are about the router. Pause the video and try them.

93
00:11:31,512 --> 00:11:39,917
Question 16 asks for the two main functions. The first is forwarding, also called switching. It is a local action.

94
00:11:40,367 --> 00:11:45,573
Forwarding moves an arriving packet from its input link to the correct output link.

95
00:11:46,023 --> 00:11:54,108
The second function is routing. Routing is a global action. It determines the source to destination paths of packets.

96
00:11:54,558 --> 00:12:00,553
Question 17. The router uses a forwarding table, also called a routing table.

97
00:12:01,003 --> 00:12:08,811
Question 18. The routing algorithm determines the paths. It creates and continually updates the routing table.

98
00:12:09,261 --> 00:12:13,506
Questions 19 to 21. Pause the video and try them.

99
00:12:13,956 --> 00:12:22,681
Question 19, store and forward. The entire packet must arrive at the router before the router can transmit it on the next link.

100
00:12:23,131 --> 00:12:27,185
So each hop adds a transmission delay of L over R.

101
00:12:27,635 --> 00:12:36,253
Question 20, packet switching. Hosts break messages into packets. Routers forward each packet from router to router.

102
00:12:36,703 --> 00:12:40,671
The packets share the links and the buffers. Nothing is reserved.

103
00:12:41,121 --> 00:12:48,951
Question 21, circuit switching. End to end resources are reserved for the call before the data transfer.

104
00:12:49,401 --> 00:12:54,819
The resources are dedicated, with no sharing, so the performance is guaranteed.

105
00:12:55,269 --> 00:12:58,533
Question 25. Pause the video and try it.

106
00:12:58,983 --> 00:13:07,453
The two techniques are F D M and T D M. In F D M, the frequencies are divided into narrow bands.

107
00:13:07,903 --> 00:13:13,215
Each call gets its own band. It transmits all the time, at the rate of that band.

108
00:13:13,665 --> 00:13:17,163
In T D M, time is divided into slots.

109
00:13:17,613 --> 00:13:24,440
Each call gets periodic slots. In its slots, it transmits at the full rate of the wider band.

110
00:13:24,890 --> 00:13:36,559
Here is the recap. One. Forwarding is local: the router moves a packet to the output link from its table. Routing is global: the routing algorithm builds the tables.

111
00:13:37,009 --> 00:13:49,425
Two. Store and forward. The router needs the whole packet first. Each hop adds L over R: 10 kilobits at 100 Mbps is 0.1 ms.

112
00:13:49,875 --> 00:13:58,003
Three. When the arrival rate is larger than R for some time, the queue grows. When the buffer is full, packets are lost.

113
00:13:58,453 --> 00:14:11,168
Four. Circuit switching reserves dedicated resources, with guaranteed performance. F D M uses bands. T D M uses slots. That is the end of video 2 a.
